  "body": "Requested by the user (2026-11-11). Grid-style fixture (tooling) plates are a common repeatable workholding base. The user runs one on their Tormach 770MX: a Saunders Machine Works 770 XL plate.\n\n**What:** a generic, parametric **fixture plate** in the fixture library, following the pattern of the built-in vises (#788/PR #802: generated mesh, parameters on `FixtureDef`, undoable insert/set commands, MCP tools).\n- **Parameters:** length, width and thickness; the hole grid (pitch in X and Y, e.g. 1 in / 25 mm / 50 mm, and the first-hole offset); thread size; an optional dowel-hole pattern (pitch and diameter); optional counterbores.\n- **Collision geometry:** the plate is a solid slab, so holes are drawn solid, which is conservative. Hole positions are kept as **named grid locations** (A1, B3, ...) for placement and shown as markers.\n- **Placement on the plate grid (the main value):** place another fixture (a built-in vise, an accessory, a mod vise, an imported STEP) **at a grid location or on a dowel pair**, as one undoable command, so a repeatable real setup is reproduced exactly. A placed fixture remembers its plate location; moving the plate moves it.\n- **Presets:** generic sizes, plus a plate sized for the Tormach 770MX table.\n  - Original geometry, never a vendor's model.\n  - The user's own 770 XL plate can be matched by entering its measured dimensions, or by importing the maker's STEP (#790).\n  - The issue records only generic figures.\n\nEffort: high (fixture geometry and placement feed the collision checks).\n\n**Done means:**\n- A plate can be placed; fixtures snap to grid holes and dowels as undoable commands.\n- Collisions are judged by the existing fixture laws.\n- Saved and loaded; MCP tools.\n- **Checked against the user's real 770 XL plate setup on their 770MX.**\n\nPart of #339.\n",
